# Ticket 02 — Successful DNS Resolution

## User Issue

A user reports that they are having problems accessing websites.

The first step is to determine whether the problem is with the internet connection itself or with DNS name resolution.

## Initial Troubleshooting

I would first check the current network configuration:

ipconfig /all

I would check the IPv4 address, default gateway, DHCP status and configured DNS server.

I would then test external connectivity:

ping 1.1.1.1

If this works, the computer can reach an external IP address without relying on DNS.

The next step is to test DNS:

nslookup bbc.co.uk

## DNS Test

I ran:

nslookup bbc.co.uk

The DNS server successfully resolved the hostname and returned several IPv4 and IPv6 addresses.

This confirmed that DNS name resolution was working.

![Successful nslookup](screenshots/ticket-01-nslookup-success.png)

## Wireshark Investigation

I captured the DNS traffic in Wireshark using the display filter:

dns

I identified the DNS query and its corresponding response.

The query was sent from the client to the DNS server asking for the IP address associated with the hostname.

The response contained valid IP addresses, confirming successful DNS resolution.

![DNS query](screenshots/ticket-01-dns-query.png)

![DNS response](screenshots/ticket-01-dns-response.png)

During another DNS exchange I investigated packets 508 and 510.

Packet 508 contained the DNS query and Wireshark identified packet 510 as its corresponding response.

The response contained several CNAME records before eventually returning IPv4 A records.

This helped me understand that a hostname does not always point directly to an IP address. DNS may follow a chain of aliases before reaching the final address.

## What I Learned

DNS translates hostnames into IP addresses that computers can use to establish connections.

A successful DNS exchange contains both a query and a response.

If a user can reach an external IP address and DNS successfully resolves hostnames, DNS is unlikely to be the cause of the website problem.

Wireshark allows me to go beyond the result shown by nslookup and inspect the actual DNS request and response at packet level.
